{title: There Is a Fountain Filled with Blood}
{artist: William Cowper}
{key: C}
{tempo: 88}
{time: 4/4}
{meta: tune CLEANSING FOUNTAIN - American camp-meeting melody (19th cent.) (1840)}
{meta: public_domain Text 1772, tune 1840: both published before 1931 (US public domain); all authors, translators and composers died over 70 years ago. Chords and section layout: original simple arrangement by Tabernacle Tools. Original verses only, no modern additions.}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
There [C]is a fountain filled with blood drawn [F]from Im[C]manuel's veins;
And sinners plunged beneath that flood lose [G]all their guilty [C]stains:
Lose all their [F]guilty [C]stains, lose all their [G]guilty [C]stains;
And sinners plunged beneath that flood lose [G]all their guilty [C]stains.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
The [C]dying thief rejoiced to see that [F]fountain [C]in his day;
And there may I, though vile as he, wash [G]all my sins a[C]way:
Wash all my [F]sins a[C]way, wash all my [G]sins a[C]way;
And there may I, though vile as he, wash [G]all my sins a[C]way.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
E'er [C]since by faith I saw the stream Thy [F]flowing [C]wounds supply,
Redeeming love has been my theme, and [G]shall be till I [C]die:
And shall be [F]till I [C]die, and shall be [G]till I [C]die;
Redeeming love has been my theme, and [G]shall be till I [C]die.
{end_of_verse}
